  <h2 id='composability'>17.5 Composability: the pieces must reassemble</h2>
  <p>
    Decomposition has a second half this chapter has so far taken for granted:
    <strong>the pieces have to go back together.</strong>
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> A composite is only truly broken down if its parts can be reassembled in JSX,
    each carrying its own capabilities and nothing else. The failure mode is the component that can only
    be used through a configuration prop: <code
      >items=&#123;[&#123; label, href, onClick, badge &#125;]&#125;</code
    >. The shape looks tidy (one component, one prop), and it usually ships in two layers that make
    it feel even tidier: a shared library component that accepts the config and iterates over it,
    and an app wrapper that builds the config. But the config prop fuses the layers right back
    together: it is markup encoded as data, and whatever
    <em>any</em> item needs, the <em>whole</em> composite now needs.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-bad'
      is:raw><span class="filename">AppNav.tsx (in @internal-ui-lib, the shared library)</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> Link <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/link"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">AppNav</span>({ items }: <span class="tok-fn">AppNavProps</span>) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* The library iterates; the entries are data. Because any entry MAY
     carry an onClick, this must be a client module, and so must anything
     that builds an items array. The fusion is in the API. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">nav</span>&gt;
      {items.map((item) =&gt;
        item.onClick ? (
          &lt;<span class="tok-fn">button</span> <span class="tok-prop">key</span>={item.label} <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={item.onClick}&gt;
            {item.label}
          &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">button</span>&gt;
        ) : (
          &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Link</span> <span class="tok-prop">key</span>={item.label} <span class="tok-prop">href</span>={item.href}&gt;
            {item.label}
            {item.badge != <span class="tok-kw">null</span> &amp;&amp; &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Badge</span>&gt;{item.badge}&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Badge</span>&gt;}
          &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Link</span>&gt;
        ),
      )}
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">nav</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    Now follow the two casualties downstream of that API, one per fusion. First the boundary: props
    that cross the server/client boundary must be serializable, and a function cannot cross (<a
      href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#children-pattern'>§5.4</a
    >). Because <em>one</em>
    item carries an <code>onClick</code>, the app wrapper that builds the array must be a client
    component, even though every other item is a plain <code>href</code>. There is no way to mark
    one entry of an array <code>"use client"</code>; the directive marks modules, and the config
    shape already fused the entries into one value. Second the data: a client component cannot
    await, so the one badge count climbs out of the nav entirely, all the way to a server parent at
    the root, which now blocks on one nav item's lookup before the nav (or anything else) renders: a
    bystander gate (<a href='14-blocking-requests.html#no-bystanders'>§14.5</a>) at the worst
    possible altitude.
  </p>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='bad' label='✕ (a) One onClick: the whole nav is client'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-bad'
          is:raw><span class="filename">MyAppNav.tsx (in @my-app-ui)</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { useState } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { AppNav } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"@internal-ui-lib"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">MyAppNav</span>({ unread }) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* Client, because ONE item needs a
     handler. The three href items did
     not ask for any of this. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [prefsOpen, setPrefsOpen] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>);

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">AppNav</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">items</span>={[
        { <span class="tok-prop">label</span>: <span class="tok-str">"Dashboard"</span>, <span class="tok-prop">href</span>: <span class="tok-str">"/dashboard"</span> },
        { <span class="tok-prop">label</span>: <span class="tok-str">"Programs"</span>, <span class="tok-prop">href</span>: <span class="tok-str">"/programs"</span> },
        {
          <span class="tok-prop">label</span>: <span class="tok-str">"Notifications"</span>,
          <span class="tok-prop">href</span>: <span class="tok-str">"/notifications"</span>,
          <span class="tok-prop">badge</span>: unread,
        },
        {
          <span class="tok-prop">label</span>: <span class="tok-str">"Preferences"</span>,
          <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>: () =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setPrefsOpen</span>(<span class="tok-kw">true</span>),
        },
      ]}
    /&gt;
  );
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
    <CompareCol kind='bad' label='✕ (b) The fetch climbs to the root'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-bad'
          is:raw><span class="filename">app/layout.tsx</span><span class="tok-kw">import</span> { getUnreadCount } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"@/server/alerts"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">RootLayout</span>({
  children,
}) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* A client component cannot await, so
     one nav item's badge count climbs
     here. The ROOT layout now blocks on
     it before the nav, or anything
     else, can render (§14.5). */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> unread = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getUnreadCount</span>();

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Shell</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">nav</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">MyAppNav</span> <span class="tok-prop">unread</span>={unread} /&gt;}
    &gt;
      {children}
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Shell</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
  </Compare>

  <p>
    This is the thesis of <a href='02-goals.html'>§2</a> failing at component scale:
    <strong
      >the granularity of your known/unknown separation is capped by the granularity of your
      composition.</strong
    > When the pieces are fused, there is nowhere to draw the boundary where known meets unknown, so links
    that were knowable at build time render as client JavaScript and wait on a root-level fetch because
    one sibling clicks and another counts (<a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#skeletons-example'
      >§11.4</a
    >). Embedding parts with different data needs and different boundary needs into one fused shape
    is a recipe for inflexibility. The fix belongs to <strong>the library's export surface</strong>:
    export the container, and export the item, <em>separately</em>, so the app can assemble and
    extend them:
  </p>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label='✓ The container, exported alone'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">AppNav.tsx (in @internal-ui-lib)</span><span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">AppNav</span>({ children }) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* No items prop, no iteration, no
     opinion about what an item is, and
     no directive: the container stays
     server-compatible. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">nav</span> <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"app-nav"</span>&gt;
      {children}
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">nav</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label='✓ The item, exported alone'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">NavItem.tsx (in @internal-ui-lib)</span><span class="tok-kw">import</span> Link <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/link"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">NavItem</span>({ href, children }) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* One item, server-compatible, with
     a children slot where a badge (or
     anything else) can land. Extend
     it; don't configure it. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Link</span> <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"nav-item"</span> <span class="tok-prop">href</span>={href}&gt;
      {children}
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Link</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
  </Compare>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">MyAppNav.tsx (in @my-app-ui)</span><span class="tok-kw">import</span> { Suspense } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { AppNav, NavItem } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"@internal-ui-lib"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { UnreadBadge } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"./UnreadBadge"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { PreferencesItem } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"./PreferencesItem"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">MyAppNav</span>() {
  <span class="tok-com">/* No directive, no data props, nothing awaited at the root. The nav is
     a server component again: the links ship static in the shell, and the
     two capabilities live in leaves below, each alone. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">AppNav</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">NavItem</span> <span class="tok-prop">href</span>=<span class="tok-str">"/dashboard"</span>&gt;Dashboard&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">NavItem</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">NavItem</span> <span class="tok-prop">href</span>=<span class="tok-str">"/programs"</span>&gt;Programs&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">NavItem</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">NavItem</span> <span class="tok-prop">href</span>=<span class="tok-str">"/notifications"</span>&gt;
        Notifications
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">BadgeDot</span> /&gt;}&gt;
          &lt;<span class="tok-fn">UnreadBadge</span> /&gt;
        &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">NavItem</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">PreferencesItem</span> /&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">AppNav</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Both bad paths reverse at once. The nav sheds its <code>"use client"</code> because the handler
      now lives in one extended item, and the root sheds its await because the badge's fetch moved into
      the one component that renders from it, behind a badge-sized boundary. The nav could equally await
      server data itself if the <em>whole</em> nav depended on it; the point is that it finally has the
      choice.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    The two extended items are each a handful of lines, and each carries exactly one capability:
  </p>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label='✓ The one unknown, isolated'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">UnreadBadge.tsx</span><span class="tok-kw">import</span> { getUnreadCount } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"@/server/alerts"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">UnreadBadge</span>() {
  <span class="tok-com">/* The nav's only data need, owned by
     the one piece that renders from it,
     suspending alone inside its item. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> count = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getUnreadCount</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (count === 0) <span class="tok-kw">return</span> <span class="tok-kw">null</span>;
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Badge</span>&gt;{count}&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Badge</span>&gt;;
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label='✓ The one client leaf, isolated'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">PreferencesItem.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { useState } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">PreferencesItem</span>() {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [open, setOpen] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>);

  <span class="tok-com">/* The app extends the library's item
     family with the nav's only
     interactivity; the sheet is
     encapsulated, §15.6-style. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">NavButton</span> <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setOpen</span>(<span class="tok-kw">true</span>)}&gt;
        Preferences
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">NavButton</span>&gt;
      {open &amp;&amp; (
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">PreferencesSheet</span>
          <span class="tok-prop">onClose</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setOpen</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>)}
        /&gt;
      )}
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
  </Compare>

  <p>
    The test to apply is simple: <strong
      >can you rebuild the composite from its exported pieces, in JSX, and put a boundary anywhere
      you need one?</strong
    > Configuration is for options; composition is for structure. A config array of plain serializable
    values (labels and hrefs) is harmless right up until an entry needs a capability: a callback, a fetch,
    a piece of client state. At that moment the entry needs to become a component, because components
    are the only unit that can carry a boundary (<a
      href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#children-pattern'>§5.4</a
    >), own a data dependency (<a href='15-minimize-the-wires.html#dialogs'>§15.6</a>), and be
    wrapped in Suspense (<a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#skeletons'>§11.4</a>) on its own.
  </p>

